==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|expost facto}}
* {{l|en|expostfacto}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|ex||from}} + {{m|la|post||after}} + {{m|la|facto|}}, ablative of {{m|la|factum||deed}}. ''Post'' takes the accusative case. (See Wikipedia for a discussion of its [[w:Ex post facto law#Grammatical form and usage|grammatical form and usage]]).

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[retroactive|Retroactive]].
## {{lb|en|legal}} [[formulate|Formulated]] or [[enacted]] after some [[event]], and then retroactively [[applied]] to it.
##* {{quote-book|lang=en|title=Retroactive Legislation|page=56|author=Daniel E. Troy|year=1998|passage=Coupled with the Constitution's proscription of '''ex post facto''' laws is a similar prohibition against bills of attainder.}}
##* {{quote-journal|lang=en|work=Barron's|date=March 21, 2009|title=Would a Tax on Bonuses Be Constitutional?|author=Jim McTague|passage=That the tax would take effect after some of the payments were made also raises issues. Says former Attorney General Richard Thornburg, also in an e-mail: "Such legislation could well run afoul of constitutional restrictions on bills of attainder, '''ex post facto''' laws, laws impairing the obligations of contract, unauthorized takings of property and the like."}}

